From 90aeb167fd5f0aefe31c9c2beac9ca41e1bd207f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jesse <jesse@jjo.ninja>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 19:01:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Do not truncate the item size when computing contentsRect()

QQuickItem::width() and height() are qreal, and under a fractional
devicePixelRatio they are routinely fractional themselves. Building a
QRect straight from them truncates, so contentsRect() comes back a pixel
short in each direction and excludes the item's last row and column.

contentsRect() is both the rect update() marks dirty for a full repaint
and the rect drawContents() fills with the background colour, so those
excluded pixels are never painted by anything. Since the item renders
into a framebuffer object -- whose contents are undefined when it is
allocated -- and the fill colour is transparent, so Qt does not clear it
either, that edge strip keeps displaying whatever happened to be in that
GPU memory for as long as the window lives.

On a 1200x2000 panel at scale 1.25 this shows up as a band of
uninitialised colour along the edge of the terminal that no amount of
output clears, and that only disappears if the window is resized.

Round the rect outward instead so it always covers the whole item.
---
 lib/TerminalDisplay.cpp | 15 ++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/lib/TerminalDisplay.cpp b/lib/TerminalDisplay.cpp
index b9768bf..2f03531 100644
--- a/lib/TerminalDisplay.cpp
+++ b/lib/TerminalDisplay.cpp
@@ -2927,7 +2927,20 @@ void TerminalDisplay::update()
 
 QRect TerminalDisplay::contentsRect() const
 {
-    return QRect(0, 0, this->width(), this->height());
+    // width() and height() are qreal and are routinely fractional under a
+    // fractional devicePixelRatio, so constructing a QRect from them directly
+    // truncates and drops the last row and column of the item.
+    //
+    // That matters more than it looks: this rect is what update() marks dirty
+    // and what drawContents() fills with the background colour, so a truncated
+    // rect leaves an edge strip that nothing ever paints. The item renders into
+    // a framebuffer object whose contents are undefined when it is allocated
+    // (see setRenderTarget() in the constructor) and the fill colour is
+    // transparent, so Qt does not clear it either -- the strip keeps showing
+    // whatever was in that GPU memory for the lifetime of the window.
+    //
+    // Round outward so the rect always covers the whole item.
+    return QRectF(0, 0, width(), height()).toAlignedRect();
 }
 
 QSize TerminalDisplay::size() const
